From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: H.265 hardware scale-down via the VE polyphase scaler

The H.265 engine has a secondary output, and mainline carries dead defines for
it that describe a power-of-two shifter like H.264's -- VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL
with H_SHIFT and V_SHIFT.  Programming it that way hangs the engine, which is
why the secondary output has never been usable here.

That is not how this part scales.  The vendor's decoder picks a scale mode per
picture, and for any real downscale it takes a mode that leaves
reg50.scale_precision at zero and instead programs a different register group:
"VE/Top1 Level", group 7, at VE + 0xf00.  It is a 4-tap/32-phase polyphase
scaler with loadable coefficients, and it lives inside the engine's existing
register window, so it needs no DT change and no separate driver.  Enabling
write_sc_rt_pic without configuring it is exactly the observed timeout: the
engine waits forever on a block that was never told its geometry.

Program it.  Input and output size, a Q12 step and its inverse per axis, a line
buffer, and 64 coefficient words -- horizontal bank first, then vertical.
Coefficients come from cedrus_scaler_coef.h, generated by
tools/video/gen-scaler-coef.py rather than lifted from any vendor binary.

10-bit works too: the secondary output needs its own 2-bit plane, so
OFFSET_ADDR_SECOND_OUT and the SECOND_2BIT fields of VE_DEC_H265_10BIT_CONFIGURE
are programmed alongside.  Those defines already existed and had never been
written.

Measured on hardware, luma PSNR against a software rescale of the same frame:

  1280x720 -> 640x360        55.45 dB
  1280x720 -> 320x180        45.01 dB
  1280x720 -> 640x180        47.85 dB
  Main10 640x480 -> 320x240  53.62 dB   (chroma U 36.10, V 31.15)

For scale, H.264's own shifter scores 46.10 dB on the first of those.  All
seven H.265 conformance vectors in the local set decode clean, scaled and
unscaled, including no-WPP, both scaling-list streams, lossless and Main10;
H.264 scaling is unaffected.

Two things here are not obvious and were each established on hardware, because
reading the vendor binary gave the opposite answer both times:

  - In the size and ratio registers the HIGH half is horizontal and the LOW
    half is vertical.  A symmetric ratio cannot tell the two orders apart, so
    this only shows up on an asymmetric scale.
  - Of the two coefficient banks, the first uploaded drives horizontal and the
    second vertical -- again invisible at a symmetric ratio, where both banks
    hold the same set.

The line buffer is sized from an observed 7680 bytes at a 1280-wide source --
six source lines, the working set of a 4-tap vertical filter.  How that scales
with width is not established, so it is sized for the widest accepted picture
with room to spare; a short buffer faults rather than corrupting quietly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0118-media-cedrus-h265-hardware-scale-down-via-the-ve-polyphase-scaler.patch (0118-media-cedrus-h265-hardware-scale-down-via-the-ve-polyphase-scaler.patch, commit a861df2)
